Now I understand the vulnerability. Let me look at the `readString` method more carefully:

In `readString()` (line ~1390-1400):
```cpp
bool OurReader::readString() {
  Char c = 0;
  while (current_ != end_) {
    c = getNextChar();
    if (c == '\\')
      getNextChar();
    else if (c == '"')
      break;
  }
  return c == '"';
}
```

The issue is: when `current_` reaches `end_` (the end pointer), the loop exits. But `end_` is set to `endDoc` which is `data_str + size` (where `size` is the original size, not decremented by 4). So the parser reads 4 bytes past the actual buffer.

But the real vulnerability is in the fuzz target: `size` is not decremented after reading the 4-byte hash_settings. So `data_str + size` points 4 bytes past the end of the actual data buffer.

